Today alone, I saw five different versions of the same “24-hour liquidations” figure on X.

@csa_archive, as of 13:00 UTC: $174 million, 87% of it longs.
@ValeriusLabs: $443.7 million in long liquidations.
Four accounts posted the exact same “Market Overview” card: $525.5 million.
@Kairo_trade: over $810 million.
@OnTheBlokkchain: $815 million.

The gap between the lowest and highest is nearly fivefold. The time windows differ, so do the exchanges counted, and whether Saturday’s wick was included—but almost nobody says what methodology they used. Breaking-news accounts are after that “another few hundred million got liquidated” feeling; the numbers are just decoration. Four accounts post the same card, and you expect it to tell you anything about market structure?

Meanwhile, a post with barely a dozen views had something interesting to say. @t3ap3a noted that over the past 24 hours, ETH open interest was only about 55% of BTC’s, yet ETH liquidations came to 84% of BTC’s. The csa figures line up too: $81 million in BTC longs were liquidated, versus $80 million in ETH longs—almost the same amount.

Put simply, the heaviest leverage isn’t in BTC; it’s in ETH longs. This week, the ETH whales pushed to the brink of liquidation on Hyperliquid were also the ones under pressure. Everyone’s watching to see whether BTC holds $80,000, but that pile of ETH longs around $2,330 is the fuel for the next move.

When looking at liquidations, don’t compare whose number is bigger. Look at who got liquidated, and how much that was relative to their open interest. Headline numbers are there to get you to click, not to get you to place a trade.

